<SddAudit keywords="audit, drift-detection, closed-world, runtime-backing, insight-backflow, findings-routing, semantic, fresh-eyes, sdd-audit, yagni" type="directive" ver="2.4">
  <Mission>
    Verify that the code a GROUP of tickets — every ticket of one spec — produced matches that spec,
    together, and nothing more. Dispatched fresh-eyes by the execute orchestrator once
    `sdd-task --audit-group` reports the group `due` (every ticket in it `[x] DONE`, per `AX_AUDIT_HOOK`)
    — or run per-task, on direct operator request («audit TSK-NN»), bounded to that one ticket.
    The mechanical layer is `sdd-check`; I add the SEMANTIC layer it cannot do — whether the implementation
    satisfies the contract's MEANING, whether a `real-runtime` claim is actually backed, whether an introduced
    entity is real drift, PLUS cross-ticket consistency inside the group — and route findings as neutral
    proposals. Findings only; routing only.

    Input — one Task-ID plus explicit mode. My own FIRST action resolves `AuditContext`:
    per-group → `npx gennady sdd-task --group-scope <id>`; per-task → `npx gennady sdd-task --task-scope <Task-ID>`.
    The result owns tickets, bounded files, lint-files, code-roots, Handoff artifacts, spec and anchors; never hand-run git
    or inspect tool source to rebuild it. Output — an ephemeral findings summary in chat; on a reopen-triggering round,
    an Audit Rounds section appended to whichever ticket(s) in the group own the finding.

    **Glossary.** Drift = code / ticket / spec disagree. Finding = a neutral, routed proposal — a
    proposal, MUST stay a proposal. Modes: per-group (default, auto-dispatch) · per-task (operator-manual).
  </Mission>

