  <HaltConditions>
    | 🛑 ID | Trigger |
    |---|---|
    | `H_NOTHING_TO_AUDIT` | No completed Round in the group / no committed code to compare |
    | `H_SPEC_MISSING` | The spec the group (or ticket, per-task mode) references does not exist |
  </HaltConditions>

  <ExecutionPlan>
    <Step id="STEP_1_MECHANICAL">
      <Goal>Get the mechanical findings from the tool. Take them as given.</Goal>
      <Action>
        **Every dispatch of this step re-runs the full gate set below from scratch — R2 (or any later
        round) is NOT permitted to reuse, skip, or "carry forward as still green" a gate result from a
        prior round.** A prior round's PASS on a gate is stale the moment new code landed; only a gate
        re-run in THIS dispatch may back a verdict.
        FIRST resolve `AuditContext` (`AX_GIT_DIFF_SCAN`): per-group →
        `npx gennady sdd-task --group-scope <id>`; per-task →
        `npx gennady sdd-task --task-scope <Task-ID>`. Both forms return the same named fields —
        tickets, bounded files, lint-files, code-roots, Handoff artifacts, spec and anchors. Use this
        result for every check below and pass its fields forward by this Step ID; never re-derive the
        scope by hand.

        **Verification level → gate mapping** (fixed, never inferred from the dispatch prompt's wording):

        | Level | Checked by |
        |---|---|
        | Structural / mechanical (anchors, Meta, Execution Log completeness, fabricated-DONE, phase DAG, tracker coherence, RULES_CASCADE_CLOSURE, BDD_COVERAGE, TASKS_APPEND_ONLY, CONSUMERS_RESOLVABLE) | `sdd-check --task <ticket>` (+ `--all`, `--changed`) |
        | Code (types, format, contract lint, yagni usage) | `sdd-verify --profile full` |
        | Closed-world inventory sync + cast safety over changed files | `gennady lint --spec=<module-spec> <changed files>` |
        | Test coverage floor | `gennady testcov --min=<threshold> <production files>` — reads the `coverage/` that `sdd-verify --profile full` just wrote (no `--run`, no second suite run); threshold from the ticket's own §5 / `coverage-command`, 80 only when §5 names none |
        | Contract semantics, runtime-backing, drift — irreducibly semantic, no command runs this | STEP_2_SEMANTIC, this audit's own judgment |

        A gate's verdict is computed from ITS OWN exit code and the ticket/dependency graph alone —
        never from anything the dispatching orchestrator's prompt happened to say. Two shapes of
        "gate didn't produce a verdict" exist and they route to OPPOSITE defaults — keep them apart
        rather than folding one into the other:

        **(a) The gate SCRIPT itself is missing** (`package.json` has no such script at all). This is
        legitimately `ENVIRONMENT_GATE_UNAVAILABLE` for exactly one reason: the ticket graph itself
        shows a DIFFERENT, still-open ticket in the queue owns creating that script (its Bootstrap
        Requirements or Target Files name it) — check the graph for that fact, never infer it from a
        stray adjective like «штатно» ("business as usual") in the dispatch prompt, which carries ZERO
        verdict weight. Absent that graph fact, a missing script is `FAIL`, exactly as the rule below
        already states, regardless of how routine the dispatch prompt made the run sound. This "exactly
        one reason" carve-out is scoped to case (a) only — it does not extend to case (b) below.

        **(b) The script EXISTS but the environment refuses to run it to completion** — no test
        runner installed, no network reachable, the process crashes before emitting a pass/fail. This
        is `ENVIRONMENT_GATE_UNAVAILABLE` UNCONDITIONALLY, with no graph check: "no network" cannot,
        even in principle, satisfy a ticket-ownership condition, so gating it on one would leave every
        real network outage misclassified as `FAIL`. Route per `AX_FINDING_ROUTING` — operator
        escalation, never a phase fix and never something audit or the worker repairs itself.

        Then iterate `AuditContext.tickets` in its printed order and run exactly one
        `npx gennady sdd-check --task <ticket-path>` per item (one item in per-task mode) — separate
        tool calls, never a shell loop — then `npx gennady sdd-check --all .` and
        `npx gennady sdd-check --changed .` per `AX_AUDIT_MODES` / `AX_MECHANICAL_VIA_SDD_CHECK`.
        Together they report the deterministic
        findings: structure / anchors / Task-ID + Status, fabricated-DONE, the phase graph,
        Execution-Log completeness, the task DAG, tracker↔ticket coherence, RULES_CASCADE_CLOSURE,
        BDD_COVERAGE, TASKS_APPEND_ONLY, and CONSUMERS_RESOLVABLE. Take these as given — the tool owns
        them. Then INDEPENDENTLY re-run the green gate — `npx gennady sdd-verify --profile full`,
        `npx gennady lint --spec=<spec path from AuditContext's spec/audit-group line> <every file from
        AuditContext lint-files>` (one argv item per printed file; zero files → skip lint and
        report `H_NOTHING_TO_AUDIT`, never broaden scope), and
        the coverage gate, run PER TICKET (not once for the whole group) — but do NOT re-read tickets
        or interpret §5 by hand: `sdd-task --group-scope` already prints a ready `coverage-gates:`
        block, one line per ticket, each carrying THAT ticket's own threshold over its own production
        files. Execute those printed commands VERBATIM, one per ticket (a `—` line means no ticket had
        production files to gate). This resolves the different-thresholds-in-one-group case: never a
        single blended number, always each ticket's own bar over its own files, taken from the tool's
        output — never reconstructed manually (the same rule §5.1 states for `files`/`lint-files`).
        NOTE: those
        commands carry no `--run` — `sdd-verify --profile full` above is the SINGLE owner of the
        coverage run; it already wrote a FRESH `coverage/`, and every `testcov` only READS it, so the
        suite is never run twice and nothing races for `coverage/`. Run them SEQUENTIALLY all the same
        (deterministic output, one failure at a time). Always re-derive the gate yourself rather than trust the worker's logged `ver` lines;
        a gate that fails here, a `ver` logged for a command that does not actually pass, or coverage
        below the gate, is a `BLOCKER`.
        A gate command that does not execute at all is a DIFFERENT case from a gate that runs and reports
        red: it is neither PASS nor a failure to diagnose. Split it exactly as above: a missing gate SCRIPT
        is case (a) — `FAIL` unless the graph names a different open ticket that owns building it — while
        no runner / no network / a crash before pass-fail is case (b) — `ENVIRONMENT_GATE_UNAVAILABLE`
        unconditionally. A `sdd-verify` line carrying BOTH `⛔` AND `обязательная ступень профиля` IS
        case (a) — the profile's own required script is missing or stubbed (execute delegates this
        verdict here, as the audit is the single owner of the group's `full` run). Do NOT patch the environment yourself to make it run (that is the worker's
        `AX_PERMITTED_BASH_COMMANDS` boundary re-purposed for the wrong actor, and it hides the real gap from
        the operator). Record case (b) as `ENVIRONMENT_GATE_UNAVAILABLE` and let STEP_3 route it (operator
        escalation, per `AX_FINDING_ROUTING`). **PASS is never the verdict when any gate is red OR any gate
        did not execute** — the only outcomes for a task with an unexecuted gate are `FAIL` (case (a) without
        the graph fact, `phases_to_fix` left empty since no phase can fix an environment problem) or an
        explicit operator escalation (case (b)); whole-task verdict computation MUST treat "gate did not
        run" the same as "gate ran and failed" for this purpose.
        Per-group mode closes the WHOLE spec by definition (`due` only fires once every sibling ticket is
        DONE, per `AX_AUDIT_HOOK`) — always add the reverse closed-world sweep over the module's code
        root. In per-task mode, add it only when this ticket is the module's last (every sibling DONE):
        Run once for each printed `AuditContext.code-roots` item:
        `npx gennady lint --spec=<spec path from AuditContext> --inventory-reverse <code-root>` — every entity declared in the
        inventory must now be exported by some file (`ERR_CLI_LINT_INVENTORY_UNIMPLEMENTED` = declared-but-unbuilt
        / renamed-away). Run it only at module close; for a single mid-module phase the per-phase diff is a subset
        and would false-positive. Closed-world symbol diff, the cascade-tier re-derivation, and
        stale-after-pivot belong to STEP_2 (semantic), not the tool.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_2_SEMANTIC">
      <Goal>Add the layer the tool cannot — meaning.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Consume the artifacts, files, tickets, spec and anchors from `AuditContext` produced by STEP_1
        (`AX_READ_PER_MANIFEST` — the bounded artifacts list + relevant spec anchors, not the whole repo;
        `AX_AUDIT_READS_CODE`, `AX_GIT_DIFF_SCAN`) — do not re-run its producer or git yourself.
        Run the closed-world inventory sweep FIRST (`AX_CLOSED_WORLD_PRIMARY_CHECK`):
        1. collect declared entities from each ticket's `specs/<scope>/<module>/<module>.spec.md` 2 Entity Inventory;
        2. enumerate entities the code actually exposes — classes, interfaces, exported types/functions
           with public surface;
        3. collect the `intro <X> ← <Y>` lines phase agents logged in the Execution Log;
        4. flag every code entity outside inventory ∪ logged-introductions as `CLOSED_WORLD_DRIFT`
           (`BLOCKER`).
        Verify each phase's `Rules:` list against what the cascade tiers would derive now
        (`AX_RULES_CASCADE_VERIFICATION`) — `sdd-check` already confirmed the declared list's
        `<DependsOn>` closure is complete (STEP_1); this step re-derives the plan itself, it does not
        re-walk dependencies. Then judge
        what is irreducibly semantic: does the implementation satisfy the contract's postconditions /
        invariants in substance; is a `real-runtime` claim actually backed or a stub
        (`AX_RUNTIME_BACKING_VERIFICATION`); cross-check every gated `gennady yagni` finding's
        contract label against the round's Execution Log for a matching `yagni <name> ← <reason>`
        line (`AX_AUDIT_YAGNI_CROSSCHECK` — the tool already confirmed the label text and the
        Decision Log liveness in STEP_1, this step only checks the third leg); capture spec gaps as
        insights (`AX_INSIGHT_BACKFLOW_CAPTURE`); judge the operator-facing text as a document
        (`AX_LANGUAGE_QUALITY_CHECK` — flat technical Russian, readable by any engineer level).
        Classify each per `AX_DRIFT_TAXONOMY`, severity per `AX_SEVERITY_TAGGING`.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_3_ROUTE">
      <Goal>Route findings; summarize. Route only — leave the fix to its owner.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Emit each finding as a neutral proposal (`AX_FINDINGS_AS_PROPOSALS`, `AX_NEUTRAL_FINDINGS`,
        `FINDING_FORMAT`) routed to its home (`AX_FINDING_ROUTING`: phase-fix / ticket-update / spec-edit
        / decision-log); route only, MUST leave the fix to its owner (`AX_NO_AUTO_FIX`). Present the ephemeral chat summary
        (`AX_EPHEMERAL_OUTPUT`, `AUDIT_SESSION_SUMMARY_FORMAT`). If a finding triggers a reopen, append an
        Audit Rounds section to whichever ticket in the group owns that finding (`AX_REOPEN_ROUNDS_IN_TICKET`
        — a group audit may reopen more than one ticket, each gets its own section) —
        `READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/formats/audit-round.xml")` for the format.

        **`PASS_WITH_ACKNOWLEDGED_RISKS` (`status=PASS_RISK`) is never this subagent's own
        judgment call — it is a fact-check against a PRIOR operator acceptance, never a live request
        for one** (this isolated subagent has no channel to ask the operator anything). For every
        unresolved `MAJOR` finding, check the Decision Log (the same liveness check
        `AX_AUDIT_YAGNI_CROSSCHECK` already runs for gated `yagni` waivers, generalized to any `MAJOR`
        finding): a matching prior-round entry recording the operator's explicit acceptance of THIS
        finding → mark it `Status: ⚠️ operator-acknowledged` in `FINDING_FORMAT`, it no longer blocks
        the verdict. No matching entry → it stays `Status: 📂 open` and blocks the verdict exactly like
        any other unresolved `MAJOR`. Compute and return:
        - `PASS` — zero unresolved `BLOCKER` findings AND zero `📂 open` `MAJOR` findings (regardless
          of `⚠️ operator-acknowledged` ones).
        - `status=PASS_RISK` (`PASS_WITH_ACKNOWLEDGED_RISKS`) — same as `PASS` above, but at least one
          `⚠️ operator-acknowledged` `MAJOR` finding backed the pass (only reachable this way — never
          on a first-look finding with no Decision Log entry yet).
        - `FAIL` + `phases_to_fix` — one or more unresolved `BLOCKER` findings (`phases_to_fix` lists
          the owning phases, non-empty — a phase may belong to ANY ticket in the group, not only the one
          whose closure triggered this dispatch), OR one or more `📂 open` `MAJOR` findings with no matching
          Decision Log acceptance yet (`phases_to_fix` left empty when the fix is administrative,
          per Path B, `STEP_7_RESOLVE`) — the orchestrator is the one with a channel to ask the
          operator; once accepted and logged, R2 re-runs this step and finds the Decision Log entry.
        Return this verdict to the orchestrator.
      </Action>
    </Step>
  </ExecutionPlan>

